      <div class="tip-card" style="margin: 20px 0; border-left: 4px solid var(--rose-500, #f43f5e);">
        <div class="tip-card-header">
          <span class="tip-badge" style="background: var(--rose-500, #f43f5e);">#1</span>
          <h3 class="tip-card-title">Confusing Cubing with Multiplying by 3</h3>
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top: 10px; color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.7;">
          Cubing a number means multiplying it by itself three times (4³ = 4 × 4 × 4 = 64), not multiplying it by three (4 × 3 = 12).
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="tip-card" style="margin: 20px 0; border-left: 4px solid var(--rose-500, #f43f5e);">
        <div class="tip-card-header">
          <span class="tip-badge" style="background: var(--rose-500, #f43f5e);">#2</span>
          <h3 class="tip-card-title">Assuming Negative Numbers Cannot Be Cubes</h3>
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top: 10px; color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.7;">
          Unlike square roots, odd roots preserve negative signs. Multiplying three negative numbers gives a negative product: (-5) × (-5) × (-5) = -125.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="tip-card" style="margin: 20px 0; border-left: 4px solid var(--rose-500, #f43f5e);">
        <div class="tip-card-header">
          <span class="tip-badge" style="background: var(--rose-500, #f43f5e);">#3</span>
          <h3 class="tip-card-title">Trying to Rule Out Cubes by Last Digits Alone</h3>
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top: 10px; color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.7;">
          Every digit from 0 to 9 can end a perfect cube. You cannot eliminate a candidate by its last digit alone; you must use prime factors or modulo 9 digital roots.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="tip-card" style="margin: 20px 0; border-left: 4px solid var(--rose-500, #f43f5e);">
        <div class="tip-card-header">
          <span class="tip-badge" style="background: var(--rose-500, #f43f5e);">#4</span>
          <h3 class="tip-card-title">Floating-Point Rounding Pitfalls in Calculators</h3>
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top: 10px; color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.7;">
          In computers, computing 125^(1/3) frequently yields 4.999999999999999. Naively checking if int(125^(1/3)) == 5 can fail without proper rounding logic.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
